<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wesley Merritt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wesley Merritt was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army general who served as a cavalry commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> and later in the wars against Native Americans of the West. In 1898 he commanded the expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in August and became the first American military governor of the islands. He then advised the American delegation negotiating the Treaty of Paris.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wesley-merritt.jpg" alt="Wesley Merritt" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Cline · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MajorGeneralWesleyMerritt_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Merritt was born in New York City and graduated from the United States Military Academy in 1860. He was commissioned a second lieutenant in the 2nd Dragoons and served at first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> under John Buford, becoming adjutant of the unit when it was renamed the 2nd Cavalry Regiment. In 1862 he was made captain and served as aide-de-camp to Brigadier General Philip St. George Cooke, then in 1863 as adjutant to Major General George Stoneman during Stoneman's Raid in the Battle of Chancellorsville.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Slightly wounded at the Battle of Brandy Station, he was appointed brigadier general of volunteers on 29 June 1863, a direct step from captain that he shared that day with Elon J. Farnsworth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">George Armstrong Custer</a>. At Gettysburg he led the Reserve Brigade in the assault on the Confederate right flank after Pickett's Charge. He took command of the 1st Division of the Cavalry Corps after Buford's death in December 1863 and fought at the Battle of Yellow Tavern.</p><p>In Major General Philip Sheridan's Valley Campaigns of 1864 his division routed the Confederate forces at the Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, then burned barns, mills and crops across the Shenandoah Valley. He was Sheridan's second-in-command in the Appomattox Campaign and one of the commissioners for the surrender at Appomattox Court House. After the war he served on the frontier, commanding Fort Davis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and later the 5th Cavalry at the Battle of Slim Buttes, and he sat on the court of inquiry into Major Marcus A. Reno's conduct at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a>.</p><p>From 1882 to 1887 he was superintendent of West Point, and as commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Missouri he sent troops into the Oklahoma District before the Land Rush of 1889. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1898 he led the Eighth Army Corps; he and Commodore George Dewey kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> unaware of their plans and arranged with Governor General Fermín Jáudenes for the Spanish garrison to surrender after token resistance. Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elwell-otis">Elwell Stephen Otis</a> relieved him on 30 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Merritt retired from the army in 1900. His first wife, Caroline Warren Merritt, died in 1893; on 24 October 1898 he married Laura Williams Caton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He died of complications of arteriosclerosis at Natural Bridge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, on 3 December 1910 and is buried at West Point Cemetery. He was portrayed in the Filipino film Heneral Luna (2015).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wesley Merritt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military governors, <b>Wesley Merritt</b>, Elwell Otis, Arthur MacArthur and Adna Chaffee, held executive and legislative power.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Natural Bridge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1884" aria-label="New York City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wesley Merritt"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4101 4034h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4101 4034h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.71,-74.01&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
